873e358adc6412781","file":"preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex","line":31,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"531d157c2c3de0c11e95236e04fbb462ce93d6b17978e37286a6af2f9c5a4457","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:78094007c9f408d873a42ba5ec1182d0c6ac33fa8787df5873e358adc6412781","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-metric-Blaschke-theorem-September-23-2026/build/sections/00-introduction.tex#L31"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:blaschke}\nLet $(M,g)$ be a connected closed smooth Riemannian manifold of\npositive dimension. If $\\inj(M,g)=\\diam(M,g)$, then $(M,g)$ is\nisometric, up to multiplication of the metric by a positive constant,\nto a round sphere, a standard real, complex or quaternionic projective\nspace, or the Cayley plane."}
